parts": ["Exactly. The value is **not autonomy for autonomy’s sake**. It is eliminating the dead time between your decisions.\n\nYou are still the orchestrator, but the system should distinguish between two very different things:\n\n- **Don judgment** — idea, approval, rejection, exception, reframe.\n- **Don clerical transport** — copy this, open that terminal, load this bootstrap, paste that handoff, move to the next seat.\n\nThe second category is where those four or five hours disappear.\n\nSo the near-term architecture should probably optimize for one thing above everything else:\n\n**resume + approve + route**\n\nMeaning if you walk away midstream, the phone should be able to tell you something like:\n\n> EC8 Quant  \n> Current gate: Luce verification complete  \n> Next route: Corrado  \n> Confidence: 97%  \n> Action: Approve / Hold / Open context\n\nYou hit **Approve** and the system continues the carousel.\n\nThat is materially different from trying to build some giant autonomous agent. It is more like a **decision queue for Don** sitting on top of isolated project runtimes.\n\nAnd the beautiful part is that the \"vomit\" side and the \"approval\" side are really just two ingress modes into the same orchestration layer:\n\n```text\nDON INPUT\n   │\n   ├── IDEA / VOICE VOMIT\n   │       ↓\n   │   classify → normalize → compile → route\n   │\n   └── APPROVAL / DECISION\n           ↓\n       resume existing state → advance route\n```\n\nThat means the mobile experience can stay stupid-simple:\n\n**Speak**\n**Approve**\n**Hold**\n**Open**\n**Redirect**\n\nEverything heavy stays behind it.\n\nThat feels like the right next abstraction: not \"mobile Command Center,\" but **Don Remote Control for the GCE runtime**."]}, "create_time": 1790297354.931694, "end_turn": true, "id": "df895dd2-8e2a-41c0-8392-a673a30dfff3", "metadata": {"can_save": true, "citations": [], "code_blocks": {"0": {"edited": false, "id": "32ma1y", "preview_language": null, "previewable": false}}, 