re / methodology review**\n\nEverything else should continue in the project runtime without dragging you back into clerical work.\n\nThe pattern becomes:\n\n```text\nPROJECT LOOP\n   ↓\nseat work\n   ↓\ngate / confidence / exception check\n   ↓\nif clean → continue carousel\nif not clean → create Don decision packet\n   ↓\nemail / Drive / lightweight mobile surface\n   ↓\nDon taps:\nApprove | Hold | Defer | Review | Redirect\n   ↓\nruntime resumes or parks\n```\n\nAnd the really important bit is the **park state**.\n\nIf something falls to 80% confidence and you’ve got bigger work moving, you should be able to hit **Defer** and the system should preserve:\n\n- what was being attempted;\n- what failed;\n- where confidence dropped;\n- what methodology or architecture was implicated;\n- what the next unresolved question is;\n- which project/workstream owns it;\n- what seat should pick it up later.\n\nThat means “slide it to the side” becomes a **first-class orchestration action**, not abandonment.\n\nThen when you revisit it, you don’t reopen a dead terminal and reconstruct history. You get a compact reconciliation packet:\n\n> **EC8 / MLB**  \n> Stopped at: Luce gate  \n> Reason: methodology deviation detected  \n> Confidence: 81%  \n> Last accepted state: X  \n> Divergence: Y  \n> Suggested next seat: Coda  \n> Actions: Review / Resume / Archive / Redirect\n\nThat’s where email and Drive are actually useful. They’re not the runtime; they’re the **notification and persistence surfaces**.\n\nSo I’d frame the stack like this:\n\n```text\nDESKTOP\nDeep work / active orchestration / Dev Pads\n\nGOOGLE DRIVE + CABLE CAR\nState / handoffs / persistence / recovery\n\nEMAIL\nAsynchronous exception delivery\n\nPHONE\nDecision surface only\n```\n\nThat is much cleaner than trying to cram a Command Center UI onto the phone.\n\nAnd it matches the way you actually work: **you stay hands-on where judgment matters, but you stop being the transport layer between every seat.**"]}, "cr